732faeaf" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561211 1080 "do not intersect" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561211 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561211 1083 #uuid "52d1d98a-2227-583b-995d-2d9f0f210a0b" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561212 1080 "do not intersect" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561212 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561212 1083 #uuid "57e75c5c-9d7e-5fbb-9e68-fd683aeb2452" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186561198 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561213 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: 5x - 2y =-1, l_{2}: -5x + 2y = 1$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561213 1072 #uuid "5c389456-1e7f-570a-b81d-ee3996221d09" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561213 1074 "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\ -5x + 2y &= 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\\n-2y &= -5x - 1 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-5x - 1}{-2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n-5x + 2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) &= 1 \\\\\n-5x + 5x + 1 &= 1 \\\\\n1 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561214 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561217 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561214 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561231 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561214 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561233 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561214 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561214 1077 "field-1" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561214 1078 #edb/ref 17592186561217 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561214 1079 #uuid "5c7459c3-7647-57df-ac62-0f53b2bd604d" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561215 1080 "do not intersect" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561215 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561215 1083 #uuid "5d633272-a745-52f8-8022-869b2818e9f9" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186561232 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561216 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: 2x - 3y = 0, l_{2}: 6x - 9y = 0$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561216 1072 #uuid "5dcff32d-252d-58bc-9fa9-cb87cd485a01" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561216 1074 "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - 3y &= 0 \\\\ 6x - 9y &= 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 3y &= 0 \\\\\n-3y &= -2x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-2x}{-3} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}x\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 9y &= 0 \\\\\n6x - 9\\left(\\frac{2}{3}x\\right) &= 0 \\\\\n6x - 6x &= 0 \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561217 1080 "do not intersect" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561217 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561217 1083 #uuid "64762017-ef57-574d-99d7-a9be2892815d" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186561209 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561218 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: -5x + y =-1, l_{2}: 10x - 2y = 2$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561218 1072 #uuid "6542765a-dbf6-5f4b-aedd-6339210b9996" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561218 1074 "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x + y &=-1 \\\\ 10x - 2y &= 2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x + y &=-1 \\\\\ny &= 5x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=5x - 1\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10x - 2y &= 2 \\\\\n10x - 2\\left(5x - 1\\right) &= 2 \\\\\n10x - 10x + 2 &= 2 \\\\\n2 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561219 1080 "intersect at infinitely many points" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561219 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561219 1083 #uuid "6f64462a-0a21-5bfe-90c2-09bf574953ca" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561220 1080 "intersect at infinitely many points" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561220 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561220 1083 #uuid "73ba1cde-65b2-5df1-8173-f771c3abe4fe" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561221 1080 "intersect at one point" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561221 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561221 1083 #uuid "75689f6e-65af-5364-b427-d0d53ea942af" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186561193 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561222 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: y = -5x - 5, l_{2}: 25x + 5y =-20$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561222 1072 #uuid "76b29f4c-d2f7-5be9-99e6-f351f6d667d5" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561222 1074 "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &= -5x - 5 \\\\ 25x + 5y &=-20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. The first equation already gives $y$ in terms of $x$:\n\n$$\ny = -5x - 5\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=-5x - 5\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25x + 5y &=-20 \\\\\n25x + 5\\left(-5x - 5\\right) &= -20 \\\\\n25x - 25x - 25 &= -20 \\\\\n-25 &= -20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always false. Therefore, we conclude that the system has no solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines do not intersect (i.e., they are parallel)." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186561207 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561223 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: -2x - 3y =-4, l_{2}: 12x + 20y = 6$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561223 1072 #uuid "787cf7fe-f2f7-5df0-b36f-e78b6273a4b5" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561223 1074 "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x - 3y &=-4 \\\\ 12x + 20y &= 6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x - 3y &=-4 \\\\\n-3y &= 2x - 4 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2x - 4}{-3} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x + 20y &= 6 \\\\\n12x + 20\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{4}{3}\\right) &= 6 \\\\\n12x - \\frac{40}{3}x + \\frac{80}{3} &= 6 \\\\\n-\\frac{4}{3}x + \\frac{80}{3} &= 6 \\\\\n-\\frac{4}{3}x &= - \\frac{62}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ is nonzero, so this equation determines exactly one value of $x$. The first equation then determines exactly one value of $y$. Therefore, the system has one solution.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at one point." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561224 1080 "intersect at one point" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561224 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561224 1083 #uuid "78dd7c65-7c23-5532-976d-ff5e37ca8e9a" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561225 1080 "intersect at infinitely many points" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561225 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561225 1083 #uuid "79766299-4422-5446-8123-6c952ce516d6" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186561208 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561226 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: -4x + y = 2, l_{2}: -12x + 3y =-15$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561226 1072 #uuid "7990b398-b163-5ff0-bd59-3b73f685ea5d" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561226 1074 "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x + y &= 2 \\\\ -12x + 3y &=-15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + y &= 2 \\\\\ny &= 4x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=4x + 2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x + 3y &=-15 \\\\\n-12x + 3\\left(4x + 2\\right) &= -15 \\\\\n-12x + 12x + 6 &= -15 \\\\\n6 &= -15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always false. Therefore, we conclude that the system has no solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines do not intersect (i.e., they are parallel)." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561227 1080 "intersect at infinitely many points" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561227 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561227 1083 #uuid "7f8afa02-2884-5634-93a8-7b7ccd61ae05" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561228 1