 nonzero, so this equation determines exactly one value of $x$. The first equation then determines exactly one value of $y$. Therefore, the system has one solution.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at one point." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186561254 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561237 1071 "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: y = -x - 4, l_{2}: -9x - 3y = 15$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561237 1072 #uuid "9a01d9c5-2885-5132-9235-1d9478baed14" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561237 1074 "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &= -x - 4 \\\\ -9x - 3y &= 15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. The first equation already gives $y$ in terms of $x$:\n\n$$\ny = -x - 4\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=-x - 4\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-9x - 3y &= 15 \\\\\n-9x - 3\\left(-x - 4\\right) &= 15 \\\\\n-9x + 3x + 12 &= 15 \\\\\n-6x + 12 &= 15 \\\\\n-6x &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ is nonzero, so this equation determines exactly one value of $x$. The first equation then determines exactly one value of $y$. Therefore, the system has one solution.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at one point." 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561238 1080 "do not intersect" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561238 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561238 1083 #uuid "9eff61d3-41d1-56c6-bd82-959956a2ca75" 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [17592186561239 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561197 13194139536047 17592186045443 true] [